---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const page = {
  title: "Create a Windows 11 bootable USB on Linux — Bootable",
  description: "Create a Windows 11 USB installer on Linux. Includes FAT32, split WIM files, setup options, and media checks.",
  ogTitle: "Create a Windows 11 bootable USB on Linux",
  ogDescription: "Create FAT32 Windows media and split oversized WIM files on Linux.",
  ogType: "article",
  current: "guide",
  doc: { label: "Safety", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/docs/safety.md" },
  crumbs: [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Guides", href: "/guides.html" }, { label: "Windows USB on Linux" }],
};
---
<Layout {...page}>
<main class="guide-main">
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={page.crumbs} />
      <article>
        <header class="guide-hero">
          <p class="eyebrow">Windows installer · Linux</p>
          <h1>Create a Windows 11 USB on Linux.</h1>
          <p class="lede">Create FAT32 installer media from a Windows ISO. Split oversized WIM files when needed.</p>
        </header>

        <div class="guide-body">
          <div class="guide-content">
            <h2 id="result">Output</h2>
            <p>Bootable creates one FAT32 partition using GPT or MBR. The installer supports UEFI, not legacy BIOS.</p>
            <p>If <code>install.wim</code> is too large for FAT32, Bootable splits it into <code>install.swm</code> parts.</p>

            <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>A trusted Windows 11 ISO.</li>
              <li>An expendable USB drive large enough for the ISO contents.</li>
              <li><code>wimtools</code> for oversized WIM payloads and CA 2023 boot files.</li>
              <li>Administrator access for the write helper.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Debian or Ubuntu:</p>
            <div class="guide-commands"><pre><code>sudo apt install wimtools
curl -fsSL https://bootable.palash.dev/install.sh | sh -s -- --gui</code></pre></div>
            <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>The USB drive will be erased.</strong> Confirm the physical drive before writing.</p></div>

            <h2 id="steps">Create the installer</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Open Bootable and choose the Windows 11 ISO.</li>
              <li>Insert the USB drive and select it by model and capacity.</li>
              <li>Choose GPT or MBR and review any Windows setup options.</li>
              <li>Review the source, drive, partition scheme, operations, and erasure warning.</li>
              <li>Confirm erasure and start the write.</li>
              <li>Wait for the media check and safe-removal result.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>The review shows every hardware, account, privacy, BitLocker, region, and policy option.</p>

            <h2 id="verification">Media checks</h2>
            <p>Before erasure, Bootable checks the installer files and FAT32 limits. After copying, it checks the UEFI files, setup payload, and split WIM parts.</p>
            <p>This does not guarantee that Windows Setup will finish.</p>

            <section class="related-guides">
              <h2>Related guides</h2>
              <ul><li><a href="/verify-bootable-usb">Verification details</a></li><li><a href="/safe-usb-writer">Drive safety</a></li><li><a href="/terminal-usb-writer">Terminal interface</a></li></ul>
            </section>
            <div class="guide-cta"><h2>Install on Linux</h2><p>Available for Linux x86-64.</p><a class="button primary" href="/download">View Linux install steps</a></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="guide-aside"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li><a href="#result">What it creates</a></li><li><a href="#requirements">Requirements</a></li><li><a href="#steps">Create the installer</a></li><li><a href="#verification">Verification</a></li></ul></aside>
        </div>
      </article>
</main>
</Layout>
